Universal pipette tips are compatible  with universal pipetters they are made  with a medical grade polypropylene  material with a high Optical Clarity to  prevent sample contamination now you  would choose low retention to minimize  sample carryover for higher  reproducibility and you choose filter  tips if you would like to prevent sample  contamination into your pipettor now  this is a requirement if you're working  with molecular assays so keep that in  mind now you choose the size based on  the amount of analyte that is being used  as well as making sure it's compatible  with the pipettor make sure to choose  tips that fit the volume Mains of your  pipetting for example 1 to 20 2 to 50 20  to 200 200 to 1000 microliters Etc  something to keep in mind you also want  to choose a size where the volume that  you're pipetting is closer to the middle  of the range of your tip so for example  if you're pipetting 25 microliters you  would want to use a 50 microliter tip  more so than a 200 microliter tip  because 50 is closer to 25 than 200.  choose sterile tips to prevent sample  contamination and non-sterile tips when  contamination is not so much of a  concern
 

